  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Transduction: the universal trick', note: 'Every sense begins with a receptor converting a physical signal (photons, pressure, chemicals) into electrical spikes. Different sensors, same output language.' },
    { math: '2 · Vision: light to brain in three stages', note: 'Photoreceptors (rods for dim light, cones for colour) → retinal processing (edges, motion, contrast) → visual cortex. Half the cortex is vision-related — sight is the brain\'s dominant input.' },
    { math: '3 · Hearing: frequency mapped physically', note: 'The cochlea is a frequency analyser: each location responds to one pitch (tonotopy — the ear is a biological Fourier transform). Hair cells convert vibration to spikes; loss is permanent (mammals don\'t regenerate them).' },
    { math: '4 · The "minor" senses are major', note: 'Touch (mechanoreceptors of varying precision — fingertips densely mapped), smell (400 receptor types, combinatorial coding — ~1 trillion detectable odours), taste (5 basic qualities), plus balance and body position (proprioception).' },
