---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/intermediate/senses-photons-to-perception">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Perception is <strong>controlled hallucination</strong> (Anil Seth's phrase): the brain
    receives sparse, noisy signals and constructs the world you experience. Each sense
    transduces a different physical quantity into the same electrical currency, and the cortex
    integrates them into one seamless model — usually accurate, occasionally fooled (illusions
    are the experiments).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the senses, from physical signal to constructed experience" widget="equationStep" config={sensesCfg} />

  <h2>What illusions reveal</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The blind spot is real and invisible:</strong> the optic disc has no receptors; the brain fills it in. Perception is construction, not recording — the fill-in is usually seamless and sometimes wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>Colour doesn't exist "out there":</strong> wavelengths exist; colour is the brain's model built from three cone comparisons. Same physics, different subjective experiences (the hard problem's doorstep).</li>
    <li><strong>McGurk effect:</strong> seeing "ga" while hearing "ba" produces the perception "da" — senses fuse before awareness. Perception is multimodal by construction.</li>
    <li><strong>Change blindness:</strong> a person swap in a video goes unnoticed. We encode far less than we think — the cognitive lesson's attention limits, demonstrated.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Seth, Being You (2021); Gregory, Eye and Brain; Simons & Chabris, "Gorillas in Our Midst" (1999).</p>
</TopicPage>
